# std.concurrency — the cross-task box + executor primitives, declared in Chezzi (phase 4c-concurrency).
#
# FILE-BACKED native module (like std/net.chz). Its native TYPES (`Shared`/`RwShared`/`Atomic`/
# `AtomicInt`/`Executor`) are ordinary members: `concurrency.Shared` after `import std.concurrency`, or
# `import Shared from std.concurrency` (an alias works). The resolver loads this real file (keeping
# the `native` marker), and the checker harvests the `native struct` declarations below as the module's
# SIGNATURE source — retiring the hand-built `native_module_sig` concurrency arm AND the bespoke
# `shared_method_sig`/`rwshared_method_sig`/`atomic_method_sig`/`executor_method_sig` method arms (their
# sigs now come from the `native fn` methods below, harvested into each type's method table — the
# phase-4c native-method-binding capability, EXTENDED here to GENERIC native structs).
#
# Every declaration is BODY-LESS: `Shared`/`RwShared`/`Atomic`/`Executor` stay the opaque VM handles
# `Ty::Shared`/`Ty::RwShared`/`Ty::Atomic`/`Ty::Executor` (NOT nominal structs — no field layout), and a
# ctor call (bare, aliased or qualified) resolves to `Resolution::Builtin` and lowers to
# `Op::NewShared`/`NewRwShared`/`NewAtomic`/`NewExecutor` (NOT off-heap host natives). The method calls stay VM-
# intercepted at runtime by name. The backends compile NOTHING for a `native fn`/`native struct` — the
# front-end reads ONLY the signature.
#
# TWO method sigs a plain harvested sig cannot express (ported as post-harvest metadata in the checker's
# `attach_native_module_metadata`): `RwShared.read(f)` recovers its return type R from the closure
# argument's return type (`read(fn(T) -> R) -> R`), so `f` is declared UNANNOTATED and the metadata step
# retypes it to `fn(T) -> ?` (any return); `Executor.submit(f)` accepts any zero-arg closure regardless
# of its return, so `f` is declared UNANNOTATED and retyped to `fn() -> ?`. `Atomic.add`/`sub` exist
# only for a numeric `T` — a dispatch-time constraint the `Ty::Atomic` arm keeps as a residual gate.

# `Shared[T]` keeps its value in a private heap of its own (TICKET-246), one user at a time.
#   - `update(f)` runs `f` INSIDE the box, on the live value, in place: `fn(t): t.hits += 1` then
#     returning `t` copies nothing. The value `f` returns is the new state.
#   - `read(f)` runs `f` inside the box too and copies only its RESULT out (`read(fn(T) -> R) -> R`,
#     `f` declared unannotated and retyped like `RwShared.read`). It runs on the live value: a
#     mutation `f` makes persists.
#   - `get()` copies the whole value out; `set(v)` copies `v` in.
# A closure given to `update` / `read` runs like the body of a spawned task: its captures are
# copies (a write to one is the task-copy error or fault, never a silent lost write) and it sees
# module globals as the caller has them at the call. `get` / `read` / `set` / `update` / printing of
# a box from inside that box's own `update` / `read` closure fault with
# `already holds the update guard`. A slow closure blocks every other user of the box.
# `StructuralEq` -- the SEALED bound an `Atomic[T]` payload parameter must carry (TICKET-250). A type
# satisfies it only when `==` on it is structural: it reaches no type that has the `eq` hook. The
# checker answers that itself, so the body is empty. `Atomic.cas` compares stored values
# structurally; the bound keeps `==` and `cas` in agreement for a payload built by substitution.
# Import-gated like `Atomic`: `import std.concurrency` or `import StructuralEq from std.concurrency`.
protocol StructuralEq:
    pass

native struct Shared[T]:
    native fn get(self) -> T
    native fn set(self, v: T) -> None
    native fn update(self, f: fn(T) -> T) -> None
    native fn read(self, f)

# `RwShared[T]` has exactly four methods for every `T`: `get`, `set`, `read`, `write`. A shared
# container is used through `std.concurrency.collection` (`ConcurrentList`, `ConcurrentMap`,
# `ConcurrentSet`, `ConcurrentCounter`), which owns the per-element readers and single-entry writers.
# A `_` name a std module declares is internal to std: the checker hides it from user modules
# (TICKET-243). The `_` container primitives of a box (`RWSHARED_VIEWS`, `src/checker/expr.rs`) are
# such names; only std calls them.
native struct RwShared[T]:
    native fn get(self) -> T
    native fn set(self, v: T) -> None
    native fn write(self, f: fn(T) -> T) -> None
    native fn read(self, f)

native struct Atomic[T]:
    native fn load(self) -> T
    native fn store(self, v: T) -> None
    native fn exchange(self, v: T) -> T
    native fn cas(self, expected: T, new: T) -> bool
    # An `int` `T` FAULTS `integer overflow in Add`/`integer overflow in Sub` on i64 overflow (same
    # checked path `AtomicInt` uses below). Go's `atomic.Int64.Add` WRAPS and never panics — a stated
    # divergence for concurrency specifically, justified by Chezzi's language-wide recoverable-overflow
    # rule (measured, Go 1.26: `add(i64::MAX, 1)` wraps to `i64::MIN`).
    native fn add(self, v: T) -> T
    native fn sub(self, v: T) -> T

# `AtomicInt` — the monomorphic, LOCK-FREE int atomic (Rust `AtomicI64` / Java `AtomicInteger` /
# Go `atomic.Int64` style). Distinct from the generic reference cell `Atomic[T]` (Mutex-backed): no
# `[T]`, nothing to widen, so it is ALWAYS a lock-free `std::sync::atomic::AtomicI64`. `add`/`sub` are
# ALWAYS valid (int is always numeric — no residual numeric gate) and keep the i64-overflow FAULT via a
# checked compare_exchange CAS-loop (NOT raw fetch_add, which wraps silently).
native struct AtomicInt:
    native fn load(self) -> int
    native fn store(self, v: int) -> None
    native fn exchange(self, v: int) -> int
    native fn cas(self, expected: int, new: int) -> bool
    native fn add(self, v: int) -> int
    native fn sub(self, v: int) -> int

native struct Executor:
    native fn shutdown(self) -> None
    # Cancel every held and running job and mark the Executor shut. It does not wait: a held job
    # never starts, a running job is cut at its next wait or loop back-edge, and each cut job's
    # `defer`s run. Each cut job's Task settles `!"task cancelled: cancel() stopped it before it
    # finished"`. `ex.cancel()` then `ex.shutdown()` waits for the cut jobs to end.
    native fn cancel(self) -> None
    # A std internal (TICKET-289): cancel the one job whose result channel is `ch` (`Task.cancel`).
    native fn _cancel_job(self, ch) -> None
    # A std internal (TICKET-219): submit `f` as a handle job whose result channel is `out`. The
    # job transition seals `out` with the cancel `!e` when it drops or cuts the job. Its only
    # caller is `submit`, with the fresh cap-1 channel it created.
    native fn _submit_settled(self, f: fn() -> None, out) -> None
    # A BODIED generic method (the first on a native struct): submit `f` and hand back its `Task[T]`.
    # `t.get()` is the only place the job's outcome appears: `?value`, or `!message` for exactly what
    # `recover:` catches, or the cancel text. The job itself never faults, so `shutdown()` raises
    # nothing for it, the run does not end, and its siblings keep running (CPython `Future.result()`).
    # A submit on a shut-down Executor returns a Task already settled with the refusal.
    fn submit[T](self, f: fn() -> T) -> Task[T]:
        ch := Channel[T!](1)
        self._submit_settled(fn(): run_outcome(f, ch), ch)
        return Task(_ch=ch, _cached=None, _ex=self)

# Chezzi closures are single-expression only, so a multi-statement job body must be a named
# top-level fn the lambda calls.
#
# `recover:` decides what becomes an `!e`; there is no second list. What it cannot catch
# (`os.exit`, `--timeout`, `--max-heap`, a deadlock verdict) ends the run with its own report.
#
# A handle job's outcome is written ONCE into its channel by a seal (TICKET-219); the first writer
# wins under the channel's lock. Two writers exist:
# 1. this body, for the value of `recover: f()`;
# 2. the job transition (`SchedCore::job_event`), for the cancel `!e` when it drops a held job
#    or a job ends cut: by `cancel()`, `t.cancel()` or a creator cancel, or ends on a fault
#    `recover:` cannot catch, after which every `defer` still runs and may read the handle (CPython
#    `shutdown(cancel_futures=True)`). An `os.exit` settles nothing: it runs no `defer`, and no
#    reader runs after it.
# A slot never carries a job's return value, so the body is the value's writer. `_submit_settled`
# and `_settle` are std internals; the only channel they seal is the fresh cap-1 one
# `submit` creates.
fn run_outcome[T](f: fn() -> T, out: Channel[T!]):
    r := recover: f()
    _settle(out, r)

# True when `v` is an airlock copy in the calling task: a write to it would fault `this value is
# this task's copy`. Reads the same predicate as that write check (`Heap::is_copied`), so std code
# can pick write-or-share without attempting the write. A scalar is never a copy (TICKET-191).
native fn is_task_copy(v) -> bool

# Returns `v` rebuilt as this task's copy (`Route::CopyRead`): the rebuild every crossing runs, so
# a write to it faults instead of vanishing, a function inside it leaves its module's globals
# unmarked, and a generator's private frame slots stay writable (TICKET-190). For std code reading
# a shared core on behalf of a handle its owner aliases (`Task.get`, a `memoize1` wrapper). Not for
# a general read path: a plain `Shared.get()` or `ch.recv()` is a handoff, and the receiver owns it.
native fn task_copy_of[T](v: T) -> T

# A std internal (TICKET-219): seal the handle channel `ch` with the outcome `v`. The first seal
# wins; a later one changes nothing. Called only by `run_outcome`.
native fn _settle(ch, v) -> None

# True once the handle channel `ch` holds its outcome. Never blocks, and never reads false after
# true: a sealed channel stays sealed. `Task.done()` reads it.
native fn is_settled(ch) -> bool

# A future handle for one `Executor.submit` job (TICKET-289; moved here from `std.concurrency.task`).
# `get()` is the ONLY place a job's fault or cancel appears; `shutdown()`, the run end and a test end
# raise none. `_ch` is the sealed cap-1 channel that holds the outcome; `_cached` holds the owner's
# outcome object; `_ex` is the submitting Executor (for `cancel()`). The `_` fields are std internals:
# user code cannot read or write them.
#
# MODEL (TICKET-219): the channel is written once by a seal: the job body seals the value of
# `recover: f()`, or the job transition seals the cancel `!e` (or the message of a fault `recover:`
# cannot catch) when it drops or cuts the job. A sealed channel's receive copies the outcome and
# takes nothing, so `get()` is `_ch.recv()` and every copy of the handle and every second waiter
# reads the same outcome: one future, many readers (CPython `Future`). `done()` never blocks and
# never reads false after true. The work STARTS at the submit and is waited for by `shutdown()` (or
# the program-exit join). The canonical shape: submit every task, `shutdown()`, then `get()` each.
#
# DETERMINISM: a Task's VALUE is `f()`; only when it runs varies. Await the tasks in a fixed
# (submission) order. There is deliberately no select-on-completion API.
#
# IDENTITY: the task that holds the original handle caches the value in `_cached`, so its repeated
# `get()` returns the SAME object. A copy of the handle (a spawned task's, an Executor job's) carries
# `_cached` as it was at the crossing; a write to it faults (D4), as for any captured value. A copy
# whose owner had not called `get()` reads a fresh copy from the channel (`task_copy_of`), so a
# write to it faults too. `is_task_copy(self)` picks between that copy and the `_cached` write.
struct Task[T]:
    _ch: Channel[T!]
    _cached: (T!)?
    _ex: Executor

    # Block until the outcome is available, then return it: `?value`, or `!message` when the job
    # faulted or `cancel()` cut it (a `T!E` in place of a raise). Idempotent.
    fn get(self) -> T!:
        match self._cached:
            ?r: return r
            None: pass
        r := self._ch.recv()
        if is_task_copy(self):
            return task_copy_of(r)
        self._cached = ?r
        return r

    # True once the job has finished, faulted or been cancelled. Never blocks, and never reads
    # false after true.
    fn done(self) -> bool:
        return is_settled(self._ch)

    # Cancel this one job. A held job never starts; a running job is cut at its next wait or loop
    # back-edge and its `defer`s run; `get()` then gives the cancel `!e`. It does not wait, and its
    # siblings are untouched. A job that already ended keeps its outcome.
    fn cancel(self):
        self._ex._cancel_job(self._ch)
